{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SchenstedMaxCorner","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.SchenstedMaxCorner","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SchenstedCornersAlong","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SYTListCorner"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.tableauShape_uninsert","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.tableauShape_uninsert","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedMaxCorner.lean#L136-L195","doc":"**The reverse cascade is shape-level corner removal.**\n\n`uninsert T i` changes exactly one row length — every row it passes through above the target is\nrewritten by `rowUninsert`, which preserves length — so on shapes it decrements entry `i` and drops\nthe row if that empties it. That is `removeCorner`.\n\nThis is the step a run needs: `IsCorner` is determined by the shape (`isCorner_iff_shape`), so once\nthe shape is tracked, a corner of `Q`'s shape is a corner of `P`'s. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.tableauShape_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.tableauShape_pos","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedMaxCorner.lean#L128-L134","doc":"A tableau with no empty rows has an all-positive shape. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.filter_ne_zero_of_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.filter_ne_zero_of_pos","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedMaxCorner.lean#L120-L126","doc":"Filtering zeros out of an all-positive list changes nothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.isCorner_of_max_in_row_sorted","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.isCorner_of_max_in_row_sorted","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedMaxCorner.lean#L102-L112","doc":"The keystone in the form a caller usually has it: the maximum is known to lie **somewhere** in\nrow `i`, and the row is weakly increasing, so it lies at the end. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.isCorner_of_max_in_row","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.isCorner_of_max_in_row","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedMaxCorner.lean#L64-L100","doc":"**A row whose last cell holds a maximum entry is a corner.**\n\nIf row `i` were not a corner, `ColStrict`'s length half — which already gives\n`len(row i+1) ≤ len(row i)` — would pin the two rows **equal in length**, putting a cell of row\n`i + 1` directly below the maximum. Column-strictness makes that cell strictly larger, contradicting\nmaximality.\n\nNote what is *not* assumed: rows need not increase strictly, and no standardness predicate appears.\n`ColStrict` and the position of the maximum are the whole input. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.le_getD_last_of_sorted","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.le_getD_last_of_sorted","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedMaxCorner.lean#L51-L62","doc":"**In a weakly-increasing row the last entry is the largest.** Brick 10's `dropLast_le_getLast`\ncovers everything before the last cell; the last cell is `≤` itself. ","signatureChunk":0}]}